From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2000
From: ponwrt bring-up <noreply@example.com>
Date: Sat, 3 Oct 2026 00:45:00 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] net: airoha: ppe: send Wi-Fi downlink to FP_WDMA, not FP_TDMA

airoha_ppe_foe_entry_prepare() programs FE_PSE_PORT_CDM4 as the egress
port for flows whose destination is a WDMA device.  On the MT7996
platforms that is right: CDM4 is the TDMA engine and the Wi-Fi firmware
drains it.

On AN7581 + MT7916D it is a different block.  The SoC-side header
airoha_wed_wdma.h puts the WED at WHNAT_WDMA_PORT 3, and the SDK
agrees -- foe_fdb_7581_64Byte.h defines

	#define FP_GDMA3	(3)
	#define FP_TDMA		(7)
	#define FP_WDMA		FP_GDMA3

and ra_nat_entry.c picks FP_WDMA (never FP_TDMA) for FOE_MAGIC_WLAN_7615
entries when SUPPORT_WDMA is set and the flow is not offloaded to the
CPU.  Nothing drains port 7 in a WED offload, so downlink packets queue
there until the PSE is exhausted.  Measured on the target: 11975 packets
stuck in the port 7 OQ, Wi-Fi dropping every 36s with no self-recovery.

Point the entry at FP_WDMA instead.  This is a per-SoC constant, not a
tunable, hence the explicit macro; AN7552 genuinely uses 7 here and is
left for whoever builds that subtarget (see
foe_fdb_7552_64Byte.h, whose FP_WDMA is "(7) /*P7 OQ10&11 => WDMA
RX0&RX1*\/").

The entry also needs the force-QoS bit.  PpeSetInfoBlk2() sets
iblk2->fqos for precisely this case -- "(SUPPORT_WDMA) && (FP_WDMA ==
fpidx)" -- and with FP_WDMA == 3 that is the branch taken.  Without it
the PPE does not steer the packet into the QDMA channel that fronts the
WDMA block, so it lands in the port 3 OQ and WED's RX_DRV never leaves
idle: measured WED_WDMA_ST 0x00000000 and RX0_PROCESSED_MIB stuck at 0
while 7281 packets sat in P3.  AIROHA_FOE_IB2_PSE_QOS (BIT(9) of info
block 2) is that same bit.

airoha_ppe_foe_flow_stats_update() also needs a second guard.  It
rewrites an entry's egress to FP_NPU so the NPU can meter the flow, and
it already refuses to touch FP_TDMA entries.  On this combination the
NPU has no Wi-Fi TX path at all -- clanker's npu_config.h leaves
HAS_NPU_WIFI_TX undefined for AN7581/MT7916 -- so redirecting an
entry that is already aimed at the WED would black-hole it.  Skip
FP_WDMA for the same reason FP_TDMA is skipped.

No PSE buffer reservation changes are needed: airoha_fe_pse_ports_init()
already reserves six queues for FE_PSE_PORT_GDM3, which is where the
WDMA RX rings land.

---
 drivers/net/ethernet/airoha/airoha_ppe.c | 45 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++--------
 1 file changed, 39 insertions(+), 7 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/net/ethernet/airoha/airoha_ppe.c b/drivers/net/ethernet/airoha/airoha_ppe.c
--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/airoha/airoha_ppe.c
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/airoha/airoha_ppe.c
@@ -23,6 +23,15 @@
  */
 DEFINE_MUTEX(flow_offload_mutex);
 static DEFINE_SPINLOCK(ppe_lock);
+
+/*
+ * PSE port that carries Wi-Fi downlink traffic into the WED/WDMA block.
+ *
+ * Mirrors the SDK's FP_WDMA, which is SoC dependent: EN7581 (and 7580/7528/
+ * 7523) use FP_GDMA3, while AN7552 uses 7. Only the former is built here,
+ * so spell the EN7581 value out rather than pretending to be generic.
+ */
+#define AIROHA_FE_PSE_PORT_WDMA	FE_PSE_PORT_GDM3
 
 static const struct rhashtable_params airoha_flow_table_params = {
 	.head_offset = offsetof(struct airoha_flow_table_entry, node),
@@ -449,9 +458,30 @@
 
 		if (!airoha_ppe_get_wdma_info(netdev, data->eth.h_dest,
 					      &info)) {
+			/*
+			 * AN7581 + MT7916D: the Wi-Fi downlink must land on
+			 * the WDMA port, i.e. FP_WDMA == FP_GDMA3 == 3.
+			 *
+			 * Upstream writes FE_PSE_PORT_CDM4 (7) here, which on
+			 * AN7581 is FP_TDMA -- the TDMA path the NPU firmware
+			 * drains. Nothing drains it when the offload is a WED
+			 * one, so packets pile up until the PSE is exhausted
+			 * (observed: 11975 packets queued, Wi-Fi dropping every
+			 * 36s with no self-recovery).
+			 *
+			 * Port 3 is the vendor value on this SoC, and three
+			 * independent sources agree:
+			 *   - airoha_wed_wdma.h: WHNAT_WDMA_PORT 3
+			 *   - SDK foe_fdb_7581_64Byte.h: FP_WDMA = FP_GDMA3
+			 *   - SDK ra_nat_entry.c: SUPPORT_WDMA selects FP_WDMA
+			 * Do not "fix" this back to 7 for AN7552, where
+			 * FP_WDMA is genuinely (7) -- see
+			 * foe_fdb_7552_64Byte.h.
+			 */
 			val |= FIELD_PREP(AIROHA_FOE_IB2_NBQ, info.idx) |
 			       FIELD_PREP(AIROHA_FOE_IB2_PSE_PORT,
-					  FE_PSE_PORT_CDM4);
+					  AIROHA_FE_PSE_PORT_WDMA) |
+			       AIROHA_FOE_IB2_PSE_QOS;
 			qdata |= FIELD_PREP(AIROHA_FOE_ACTDP, info.bss);
 			wlan_etype = FIELD_PREP(AIROHA_FOE_MAC_WDMA_BAND,
 						info.idx) |
@@ -778,7 +807,24 @@
 	}
 
 	pse_port = FIELD_GET(AIROHA_FOE_IB2_PSE_PORT, *ib2);
-	if (pse_port == FE_PSE_PORT_CDM4)
+
+	/*
+	 * Never re-point an entry that already targets a Wi-Fi offload
+	 * engine. Everything below rewrites the egress to FP_NPU (6) so the
+	 * NPU can forward it with per-flow metering. That is correct for
+	 * GDM ports, but the two Wi-Fi destinations must be left alone:
+	 *
+	 *   - FP_TDMA (7): on MT7996 the Wi-Fi firmware drains it. Upstream
+	 *     already skips it, and that behaviour is kept.
+	 *   - FP_WDMA (3): on AN7581 + MT7916 the WED/WDMA block drains it
+	 *     (see airoha_ppe_foe_entry_prepare()). The MT7916 NPU firmware
+	 *     has no TX path at all -- HAS_NPU_WIFI_TX is not defined for
+	 *     this combination -- so redirecting to port 6 would black-hole
+	 *     the flow and re-create the PSE exhaustion this driver was
+	 *     fixed for.
+	 */
+	if (pse_port == FE_PSE_PORT_CDM4 ||
+	    pse_port == AIROHA_FE_PSE_PORT_WDMA)
 		return;
 
 	airoha_ppe_foe_flow_stat_entry_reset(ppe, npu, index);
-- 
